    "long": {
      "text": "The river runs north for about forty miles before it turns toward the coast. In spring the water rises quickly, and the old bridge near the mill is often closed for several days. People who live on the far side plan ahead: they stock up on groceries, park their cars on the high road, and walk the last half mile with flashlights. Most years nobody minds. It is quieter then, and the town feels smaller, as if everyone has agreed to slow down for a while. When the water finally drops, the bridge reopens with no ceremony at all, and by the end of the week it is as if nothing happened.",
